1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,640
They're the dark achievements of our intellect.

2
00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:11,480
Nuclear, chemical, biological, and even dirty bombs.

3
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:16,420
They're nightmare weapons designed to kill indiscriminately.

4
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:20,420
And they may be in the hands of terrorists.

5
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:23,860
From furnaces burning forgotten poisons,

6
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:27,460
to machines that probe, detect, and analyze.

7
00:00:27,461 --> 00:00:31,340
technology is on the front line of defense

8
00:00:31,341 --> 00:00:33,340
now

9
00:00:33,341 --> 00:00:35,540
weapons of mass destruction

10
00:00:35,541 --> 00:00:37,220
on modern marvels

11
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:51,500
we now return to weapons of mass destruction

12
00:00:51,501 --> 00:00:53,220
on modern marvels

13
00:00:53,221 --> 00:01:02,280
Nestled in the majestic Rocky Mountains is the small town of Golden, Colorado.

14
00:01:04,020 --> 00:01:09,460
It was here on October 8, 2006 that the alarm went off.

15
00:01:09,461 --> 00:01:23,461
This is the primary center on Earth for monitoring earthquake activity anywhere in the world.

16
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:27,820
And we do this 24-7.

17
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:35,780
Over 500 seismic stations around the world are connected to the National Earthquake Information Center in Colorado.

18
00:01:35,781 --> 00:01:40,780
When the Earth trembles, this is the epicenter for information.

19
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:46,780
The media and the world expects an answer from us, and they expect it very quickly.

20
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:55,700
Unknown to the analysts, North Korea had just detonated a nuclear weapon of mass destruction in an underground test.

21
00:01:55,701 --> 00:02:04,820
There was a lot of pressure on our analysts, and because of that, the phones were just

22
00:02:04,821 --> 00:02:06,340
simply ringing off the hook.

23
00:02:06,341 --> 00:02:11,780
It was pretty clear by looking at the signals that it was an explosion and not an earthquake.

24
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:16,560
An earthquake creates two distinct shockwaves.

25
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:22,360
Here we have an earthquake, and on this seismogram, we can see the primary wave, which is shown

26
00:02:22,361 --> 00:02:29,800
here and later on we see the secondary wave which is shown here now here's the actual north korean

27
00:02:29,801 --> 00:02:38,760
explosion shown here and what you see here is you see the primary wave and later on you don't see

28
00:02:38,761 --> 00:02:46,840
any real evidence of a secondary wave the earthquake center confirmed the blast hours before the north

29
00:02:46,841 --> 00:02:51,840
The Korean government announced it had performed a nuclear bomb test.

30
00:02:51,841 --> 00:02:54,340
But a question remained.

31
00:02:54,341 --> 00:02:57,740
How big was the weapon?

32
00:02:57,741 --> 00:03:02,840
So using information and size of grams like this, we were able to determine that the magnitude

33
00:03:02,841 --> 00:03:07,760
of the event was about a magnitude 4.2.

34
00:03:07,761 --> 00:03:13,640
This event was comparable in size to many of the explosions in the mining areas of the

35
00:03:13,641 --> 00:03:16,640
U.S. that we record all the time.

36
00:03:16,641 --> 00:03:26,640
Another government agency, the Air Force Technical Application Center, calculated the North Korean blast to be one-half kiloton.

37
00:03:26,641 --> 00:03:34,640
By comparison, the Hiroshima blast in 1945 was between 13 and 15 kilotons.

38
00:03:34,641 --> 00:03:44,640
Many nuclear warheads built by the United States and Russia today are one megaton, 2,000 times larger than the North Korean bomb.

39
00:03:44,641 --> 00:03:52,560
A half a kiloton of yield is something like a large tractor-trailer truck completely filled with high explosive.

40
00:03:52,980 --> 00:03:53,800
Not a lot of yield.

41
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:58,100
As a nuclear weapons designer myself, I would guess that somehow they screwed it up.

42
00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:00,460
Personally, it sounds like a failure to me.

43
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:07,080
Even if the North Korean test fizzled, the country has joined the nuclear club,

44
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:10,520
increasing the global fear of weapons of mass destruction.

45
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:24,700
We certainly should be concerned about the specter or the likelihood of a terrorist organization like Al-Qaeda attempting to get or use weapons of mass destruction.

46
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:31,480
By definition, WMDs are designed to kill large numbers of people indiscriminately.

47
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:38,320
They're usually categorized as nuclear, biological, or chemical.

48
00:04:38,321 --> 00:04:46,580
Some add hypothetical dirty bombs, which would be conventional explosives wrapped in radioactive material.

49
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:55,860
And when terrorists are willing to die in an attack, even a jetliner can become a WMD.

50
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:05,420
We see new methods of delivery working where terrorists could transit nuclear or biological or chemical materials into the United States

51
00:05:05,421 --> 00:05:09,320
through a variety of channels that would be very difficult to detect.

52
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:15,620
The expression weapons of mass destruction was coined in 1937.

53
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:20,120
It referred to the aerial bombardment of Guernica, Spain,

54
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:21,680
by the German Luftwaffe.

55
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:27,300
The conventional bombing was so heavy,

56
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:30,960
it created a firestorm, virtually destroying the town.

57
00:05:30,961 --> 00:05:37,880
Today, a nuclear bomb is the most powerful WMD.

58
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:43,420
Nations that are known to have nuclear weapons of mass destruction are the United States,

59
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:52,140
members of the former Soviet Union, China, Great Britain, France, India, Pakistan, and North Korea.

60
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:58,380
Additionally, Israel is strongly suspected to possess an arsenal of up to 200 nuclear weapons.

61
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:04,560
And the United Nations has accused Iran of developing a nuclear bomb.

62
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:15,060
Now, terrorists may not be far behind in acquiring the bomb.

63
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:22,260
Al-Qaeda definitely has an interest in acquiring and using weapons of mass destruction,

64
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:23,860
in particular, nuclear weapons.

65
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:28,200
And in fact, particular religious figures close to Al-Qaeda have actually issued fatwas,

66
00:06:28,201 --> 00:06:33,160
as religious statements that authorize the use of nuclear weapons against Americans.

67
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:39,880
So how did North Korea, virtually bankrupt and isolated from the rest of the world,

68
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:41,840
manage to produce a nuclear weapon?

69
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:48,280
The general ideas of how a nuclear weapon works, how to build a nuclear weapon,

70
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:52,560
are out in the open literature on the Internet, various places.

71
00:06:52,561 --> 00:06:57,060
The details of how to make a nuclear weapon work are not necessarily there.

72
00:06:57,061 --> 00:07:03,740
the biggest impediment to making a nuclear weapon is getting nuclear material

73
00:07:05,740 --> 00:07:10,960
after years of intensive work shrouded in secrecy the United States was the

74
00:07:10,961 --> 00:07:15,740
first nation to successfully detonate an atomic bomb

75
00:07:15,741 --> 00:07:27,860
At that time, the closely guarded secret upon which the experiment depended was uranium

76
00:07:27,861 --> 00:07:30,800
enrichment.

77
00:07:30,801 --> 00:07:36,020
The enrichment process separates heavier from lighter uranium atoms.

78
00:07:36,021 --> 00:07:41,360
The lighter atoms, called uranium-235, are more unstable.

79
00:07:41,361 --> 00:07:46,640
Here are the atoms that are split in a nuclear explosion.

80
00:07:46,641 --> 00:07:51,660
During the Manhattan Project, the most significant method developed for separating uranium was

81
00:07:51,661 --> 00:07:54,780
gaseous diffusion.

82
00:07:54,781 --> 00:08:00,780
The United States built massive facilities to produce enriched uranium by this method.

83
00:08:00,781 --> 00:08:05,800
Inside these processing plants, uranium gas was filtered through a series of semi-permeable

84
00:08:05,801 --> 00:08:07,000
membranes.

85
00:08:07,001 --> 00:08:11,240
But gaseous diffusion isn't the only way to enrich uranium.

86
00:08:11,241 --> 00:08:16,280
Here's another method that's cheaper, more efficient and much more widely available.

87
00:08:16,281 --> 00:08:23,000
It employs a series of gas centrifuges and produces the same result for 1 20th of the energy costs.

88
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:31,840
The North Koreans acquired centrifuge technology from Dr. Abdul Qadir Khan,

89
00:08:31,841 --> 00:08:36,560
a Pakistani scientist and the founder of Pakistan's nuclear program.

90
00:08:36,561 --> 00:08:40,840
In 2004, he confessed to giving away the secret and is now in prison.

91
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:48,580
A centrifuge is a cylinder filled with uranium gas, spinning at 90,000 rpms.

92
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:54,280
This forces the heavier atoms to the outside of the cylinder.

93
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:03,580
Only seven uranium atoms in a thousand are the lighter weight uranium-235, which remain on the inside.

94
00:09:03,581 --> 00:09:07,680
The heavier ones are separated out.

95
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:14,340
1,000 to 3,000 centrifuges operating constantly for one year

96
00:09:14,341 --> 00:09:16,900
might produce a pound of enriched uranium,

97
00:09:17,620 --> 00:09:19,540
enough to make one bomb.

98
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:23,620
You have to enrich, and then you enrich, and then you enrich,

99
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:27,300
and you go through a stream, a process of these,

100
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:28,440
over and over and over again

101
00:09:28,441 --> 00:09:30,620
to get to very high enrichment levels.

102
00:09:30,621 --> 00:09:38,640
Once enriched, the unstable uranium atoms must be kept in separate subcritical masses to make a bomb.

103
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:46,560
To activate the device, a conventional explosive is used as a trigger, rapidly smashing the two masses together.

104
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:51,840
This creates a critical mass and causes a runaway fission chain reaction.

105
00:09:52,840 --> 00:09:58,020
As the atoms split, they convert matter into energy, and the explosion is born.

106
00:09:58,021 --> 00:10:02,260
The longer the reaction lasts, the bigger the blast.

107
00:10:03,420 --> 00:10:05,980
There are many ways to make a nuclear weapon fail.

108
00:10:05,981 --> 00:10:08,860
There are very few ways to make a nuclear weapon work well.

109
00:10:10,140 --> 00:10:13,880
A nuclear bomb may fail if the uranium is not enriched properly,

110
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:15,960
the trigger device malfunctions,

111
00:10:16,860 --> 00:10:20,700
or the critical mass is not maintained for a sufficient period of time.

112
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:23,080
Testing is paramount.

113
00:10:23,081 --> 00:10:31,880
Much of the technology behind underground testing was developed here, at the Nevada test

114
00:10:31,881 --> 00:10:33,580
site just north of Las Vegas.

115
00:10:33,581 --> 00:10:39,960
We're sitting here in the middle of the Nevada desert at Bilby Crater.

116
00:10:39,961 --> 00:10:44,820
This was an underground nuclear test conducted 2,400 feet below the surface.

117
00:10:44,821 --> 00:10:52,060
As you can see, a huge crater was created, over 1,800 feet across and 80 feet deep.

118
00:10:52,061 --> 00:10:56,000
This crater today is just as it was in 1963.

119
00:10:58,040 --> 00:11:04,000
The United States has detonated over 920 nuclear weapons underground at the Nevada testing site.

120
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:12,120
To test a weapon, scientists drilled a shaft to a depth of more than a thousand feet.

121
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:16,960
They attached a diagnostic cable to the bomb as it was lowered down.

122
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:19,180
Then they sealed the shaft.

123
00:11:22,060 --> 00:11:29,380
The explosion from the device caused the rock and sand above it to liquefy instantly, which created a crater.

124
00:11:31,120 --> 00:11:36,380
The blast data was sent back through the diagnostic cable milliseconds before it was destroyed.

125
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:44,555
In 1992, in an effort to halt nuclear proliferation, President George Bush Sr.

126
00:11:44,556 --> 00:11:48,380
declared a moratorium on all underground nuclear testing.

127
00:11:48,381 --> 00:11:55,740
Since then, scientists at the Nevada test site have devised new testing technology

128
00:11:55,741 --> 00:11:58,980
to ensure the reliability of the U.S. nuclear arsenal,

129
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:03,440
including some weapons over 45 years old.

130
00:12:04,820 --> 00:12:07,580
Since the moratorium on nuclear testing in 1992,

131
00:12:08,140 --> 00:12:10,860
we can no longer go out and test a nuclear device,

132
00:12:10,861 --> 00:12:15,140
so we rely on computer models to simulate these nuclear detonations.

133
00:12:15,141 --> 00:12:23,260
To test the older weapons, scientists dismantle a representative nuclear bomb and extract some plutonium.

134
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:29,680
They place it on a target at the end of this enormous gun, called the Jasper.

135
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:32,860
Five, three, two, one.

136
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:34,320
Normal light fire.

137
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:38,380
The Jasper is loaded from the breech.

138
00:12:38,381 --> 00:12:46,980
This piston is inserted into the launch tube, and then we add a propellant charge and close off our breach.

139
00:12:47,460 --> 00:12:53,720
We then connect a detonator to the breach that will ignite the propellant inside

140
00:12:53,721 --> 00:12:58,040
and create explosive gases that will drive this piston down the pump tube.

141
00:13:01,380 --> 00:13:03,800
The Jasper fires in two stages.

142
00:13:04,580 --> 00:13:07,440
First, as the piston is driven down the tube,

143
00:13:07,960 --> 00:13:12,560
it heats and compresses hydrogen gas to 10,000 pounds per square inch.

144
00:13:13,980 --> 00:13:18,140
In stage two, the pressurized gas drives a projectile

145
00:13:18,141 --> 00:13:20,780
down the launch tube at 8,000 miles an hour

146
00:13:20,781 --> 00:13:23,740
toward a plutonium target.

147
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,500
The projectile's impact produces a shockwave through the target

148
00:13:28,501 --> 00:13:29,960
in a millionth of a second.

149
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:33,540
We can actually take an X-ray of the plutonium

150
00:13:33,541 --> 00:13:36,300
at a billionth of a second at the exact time interval

151
00:13:36,301 --> 00:13:38,920
where we want to see that shockwave traveling through it.

152
00:13:40,660 --> 00:13:44,300
By measuring how the shockwave travels through the old bomb's plutonium

153
00:13:44,301 --> 00:13:46,400
and comparing it to new plutonium,

154
00:13:47,220 --> 00:13:50,520
scientists can determine the safety and reliability of the older weapons.

155
00:13:51,640 --> 00:13:55,020
I sleep a lot better at night knowing that our existing stockpile

156
00:13:55,021 --> 00:13:57,260
is going to be safe, secure, and reliable.

157
00:13:59,260 --> 00:14:02,800
With time and money, most nations, like North Korea,

158
00:14:02,801 --> 00:14:04,200
can produce a bomb.

159
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:08,060
But for a terrorist organization, it's a different story.

160
00:14:09,220 --> 00:14:11,900
They're more likely to buy or steal one.

161
00:14:12,840 --> 00:14:16,680
The concern after the Cold War was that there would be loose nukes,

162
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:20,500
that the large number, tens of thousands of nuclear weapons

163
00:14:20,501 --> 00:14:22,180
that the Soviet Union possessed

164
00:14:22,181 --> 00:14:24,600
would somehow get into the wrong hands

165
00:14:24,601 --> 00:14:27,480
and be used in unexpected ways.

166
00:14:27,481 --> 00:14:34,740
Although the nuclear stockpiles of the United States and the former Soviet Union

167
00:14:34,741 --> 00:14:39,140
have been reduced by 43,000 warheads since the end of the Cold War,

168
00:14:39,560 --> 00:14:43,320
more than 7,000 nuclear weapons still exist in Russia today.

169
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:49,260
That country's stockpile isn't nearly as secure as the U.S. arsenal.

170
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:56,400
A terrorist would need a softball-sized chunk of nuclear material

171
00:14:56,401 --> 00:15:00,240
to create a weapon with the power of the Hiroshima bomb of 1945.

172
00:15:01,960 --> 00:15:06,040
It would have the capability of probably destroying everything

173
00:15:06,041 --> 00:15:09,360
within a mile-and-a-half radius of the point of detonation.

174
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:12,580
It might kill anywhere from 50 to half a million individuals.

175
00:15:15,660 --> 00:15:18,480
Some tactical nuclear weapons are quite small

176
00:15:18,481 --> 00:15:19,980
and could be smuggled into a country.

177
00:15:21,540 --> 00:15:25,480
Each year, more than 10 million cargo containers enter the United States.

178
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:29,740
Any one of them could contain a weapon of mass destruction.

179
00:15:31,500 --> 00:15:37,880
To meet this threat, the U.S. depends on new technology to safeguard its seaports.

180
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:46,780
We now return to Weapons of Mass Destruction on Modern Marvels.

181
00:15:56,400 --> 00:16:01,300
The Los Angeles and Long Beach harbors combined represent the busiest seaport in the United States.

182
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:08,540
7.5 million 20-foot containers pass through this port every year.

183
00:16:09,780 --> 00:16:13,380
The true nightmare would be the entry of a nuclear device.

184
00:16:14,580 --> 00:16:20,520
A terrorist organization might have easier access to another worrisome weapon called a dirty bomb.

185
00:16:20,521 --> 00:16:34,521
Known as a radiological dispersion device, a dirty bomb combines conventional explosives, such as dynamite, with radioactive materials in the form of powder or pellets.

186
00:16:38,340 --> 00:16:41,360
The radioactive material could be a waste product.

187
00:16:41,361 --> 00:16:48,180
There are a variety of sources, including nuclear batteries from unmanned Russian lighthouses

188
00:16:48,181 --> 00:16:54,200
and spent radioactive fuel from reactors inside decommissioned Russian submarines.

189
00:16:58,480 --> 00:17:03,040
A dirty bomb won't create the fission reaction required for a nuclear explosion,

190
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:07,560
but it will spread radioactive components.

191
00:17:07,561 --> 00:17:15,160
wind and weather conditions would determine how the deadly radioactive cloud is spread

192
00:17:15,161 --> 00:17:23,100
one of the most sophisticated weather forecasting centers in the united states

193
00:17:23,101 --> 00:17:26,880
is located at the national atmospheric release advisory center

194
00:17:26,881 --> 00:17:30,120
at lawrence livermore laboratory in northern california

195
00:17:30,121 --> 00:17:35,700
it develops predictive models for wmd attacks on american cities

196
00:17:35,701 --> 00:17:43,720
If there's a threat of a radiological explosion, the center gets to work.

197
00:17:43,721 --> 00:17:46,900
We need to map the contamination where it's going to spread in the atmosphere.

198
00:17:47,880 --> 00:17:52,320
This is a very important tool for forecasting the dispersion of material very early during an incident,

199
00:17:52,700 --> 00:17:55,140
when lives are at stake and people may be affected.

200
00:17:55,480 --> 00:17:58,869
We expect everything to drift out towards the coast from downtown L.A.

201
00:17:58,870 --> 00:17:59,740
for this exercise.

202
00:17:59,960 --> 00:18:00,660
Okay.

203
00:18:00,661 --> 00:18:04,800
In a simulated dirty bomb attack on Los Angeles,

204
00:18:05,080 --> 00:18:08,200
the lab's computers determine how radiation is spread.

205
00:18:09,060 --> 00:18:13,340
This is a hypothetical radiological dispersal device near downtown Los Angeles.

206
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:15,200
The location of the explosion is right here,

207
00:18:15,260 --> 00:18:17,920
and you can see the winds are carrying the material towards the west.

208
00:18:18,280 --> 00:18:20,020
This is a potentially affected area.

209
00:18:23,760 --> 00:18:25,640
In this hypothetical attack,

210
00:18:26,460 --> 00:18:28,800
downtown Los Angeles suffers immediate deaths

211
00:18:28,801 --> 00:18:31,100
and property damage from the explosion itself.

212
00:18:32,400 --> 00:18:35,680
A radioactive dust cloud extends several miles

213
00:18:35,681 --> 00:18:37,600
beyond the city center into Hollywood.

214
00:18:39,720 --> 00:18:43,160
Because radioactive materials release subatomic particles

215
00:18:43,161 --> 00:18:46,380
that could cause abnormal chemical reactions inside cells,

216
00:18:47,620 --> 00:18:51,180
people close to the blast could suffer immediate radiation sickness,

217
00:18:51,900 --> 00:18:56,200
while others nearby might contract cancer years after the event.

218
00:18:56,201 --> 00:19:02,240
You need to forecast where the material is going to go quickly so responders know how to approach the scene.

219
00:19:02,580 --> 00:19:07,660
They know what neighborhoods, what areas of the city might be affected so they can issue sheltering in place recommendations.

220
00:19:09,780 --> 00:19:15,020
Of course, the best plan is to prevent a dirty bomb or other WMD from ever entering the country.

221
00:19:16,980 --> 00:19:19,680
That's where other newly developed technology comes in.

222
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:26,060
The LAC port complex employs a multi-layered approach to security.

223
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:32,440
The first line of defense is to search inside shipping containers as soon as they are unloaded from the ships.

224
00:19:33,880 --> 00:19:37,600
With a vast number of containers, it's impossible to hand search each one.

225
00:19:39,800 --> 00:19:43,560
Instead, a mobile gamma scanning device is brought dockside.

226
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:50,020
Using a gamma beam shot through the container, it takes an X-ray of the contents.

227
00:19:50,960 --> 00:19:53,200
The process takes only a matter of seconds.

228
00:19:53,201 --> 00:19:57,520
The mobile gamut imaging system is a real force multiplier for us.

229
00:19:57,521 --> 00:20:01,840
All the containers that we want to look at can seamlessly go through the detection.

230
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:05,126
You can actually see our next commodity.

231
00:20:05,127 --> 00:20:06,880
We're supposed to be looking for oil and drums

232
00:20:07,440 --> 00:20:08,362
coming from France.

233
